                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^2
            \-groups are equivalent to
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+square"} crossed square
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Note that to calculate a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
                it is better to work with crossed squares, but to prove something is a colimit, it is better to work with
                = succeed "-groups." do
                  %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_21.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi cat
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^2
            %li
              %p
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_22.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi cat
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^2
                \-groups are a special case of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cat-n-group"} cat-n-groups
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_23.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-fold+category"} fold groupoids
                in the
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_24.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Grp
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp
            %li
              %p
                The use of
                = succeed "-fold" do
                  %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_25.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_26.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Grp
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp
                rather than
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_27.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groupoid"} groupoids
                means that information contained in the ‘model’ is more ‘spread out’, but is often
                %em repeated
                in different forms in different parts of the model.
            %li
              %p
                The homotopical example found by Loday can be seen as follows. Let
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_28.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ;
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X;A,B)
                be a pointed triad, so that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_29.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B \subseteq X
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_30.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Φ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
                be the space of maps
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_31.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 3
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^3 \to X
                which map the faces of direction
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_32.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                to the base point, the faces of direction
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_33.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
                into
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_34.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                and the faces of direction
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_35.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
                into
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_36.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_37.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                        %mo =
                        %mi Π
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ;
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Φ
                        %mo ,
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = \Pi'(X;A,B)= \pi_1(\Phi,*)
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_38.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                is certainly a group. The surprise is that the compositions of cubes in directions
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_39.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
                and
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_40.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
                are inherited by
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_41.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                to make it a
                = succeed "-group." do
                  %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_42.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi cat
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^2
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+square"} crossed square
                is the classical one
                %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_43.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ;
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X;A,B)
                involving triad homotopy groups.
          %p
            Loday gives the more general description in terms of squares of spaces and the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_44.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi cat
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^2
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_45.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            It is not completely clear how best to deal with the many-pointed case, but working within categories of groupoids with a given set,
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_f1b74c58e2d4effec1cdfabaef50fb11b113af97_46.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibered+category"} fibered category
            situation.
          %h1#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p J.-L. Loday, Spaces with finitely many homotopy groups, J.Pure Appl. Alg., 24 (1982), 179–202.
            %li
              %p R. Brown and J.-L. Loday, Van Kampen theorems for diagrams of spaces, Topology, 26 (1987), 311–337.
        .revisedby
